{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_11-003j-methods-of-policy-analysis-sprin_assignments","statement":"The course is 11.003J Methods of Policy Analysis, taught in Spring 2016 at the undergraduate level.","source_span":"11.003J | Spring 2016 | Undergraduate\nMethods of Policy Analysis"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_11-003j-methods-of-policy-analysis-sprin_assignments","statement":"The instructor of the course is Cherie Miot Abbanat.","source_span":"Instructor\nCherie Miot Abbanat"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_11-003j-methods-of-policy-analysis-sprin_assignments","statement":"Assignment 4 is an open-ended assignment due at Session 17.","source_span":"Due Session 17\nOpen-ended"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_11-003j-methods-of-policy-analysis-sprin_assignments","statement":"Students may choose one of two options for Assignment 4 and will only get credit for doing one, though they may do both if useful.","source_span":"you may choose one of the following options. You'll only get credit for doing one, but you may do both if you find them both to be of use to you"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_11-003j-methods-of-policy-analysis-sprin_assignments","statement":"Option 1 requires writing a two-page draft of the student's final project idea for review.","source_span":"Write a two-page draft of your final project idea for review."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_11-003j-methods-of-policy-analysis-sprin_assignments","statement":"Option 2 requires re-writing one of the first three assignments based on feedback provided and the student's own development over the semester.","source_span":"Re-write one of your first three assignments based on the feedback we provided and your own development over the course of the semester."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_11-003j-methods-of-policy-analysis-sprin_assignments","statement":"For Option 2, if the quality of the rewritten work improves, a higher grade is assigned, but it counts for Assignment 4 rather than replacing the original lower grade.","source_span":"If the quality of your work (e.g. argument and writing) improves, we will assign a higher grade. This grade won't replace the lower one, but rather will count for Assignment 4."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_11-003j-methods-of-policy-analysis-sprin_assignments","statement":"If a student revises an assignment that was originally completed as part of a partner or group project, they must still make the revisions on their own.","source_span":"If you choose to revise an assignment originally completed as part of a partner or group project, you will still need to make revisions on your own."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d1_11-003j-methods-of-policy-analysis-sprin_assignments","fact_refs":[0,1],"question":"Who teaches 11.003J Methods of Policy Analysis and in what semester was it offered according to this document?","answer":"Cherie Miot Abbanat teaches 11.003J Methods of Policy Analysis, which was taught in Spring 2016 at the undergraduate level."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d1_11-003j-methods-of-policy-analysis-sprin_assignments","fact_refs":[2],"question":"When is Assignment 4 for Methods of Policy Analysis due?","answer":"Assignment 4, an open-ended assignment, is due at Session 17."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d1_11-003j-methods-of-policy-analysis-sprin_assignments","fact_refs":[3],"question":"Can a student get credit for completing both options of Assignment 4 in Methods of Policy Analysis?","answer":"No, students only get credit for doing one option, though they may still complete both if they find it useful."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d1_11-003j-methods-of-policy-analysis-sprin_assignments","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What does Option 1 of Assignment 4 require students to write?","answer":"Option 1 requires students to write a two-page draft of their final project idea for review."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d1_11-003j-methods-of-policy-analysis-sprin_assignments","fact_refs":[6],"question":"If a student improves their work by rewriting a previous assignment under Option 2, does the new grade replace the old one?","answer":"No, the higher grade does not replace the lower one; instead it counts as the grade for Assignment 4."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d1_11-003j-methods-of-policy-analysis-sprin_assignments","fact_refs":[7],"question":"What must a student do if they want to revise an assignment for Assignment 4 that was originally a partner or group project?","answer":"They must make the revisions on their own, even if the original assignment was completed as part of a partner or group project."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_11-005-introduction-to-international-dev_assignments","statement":"The course is 11.005 Introduction to International Development, taught Spring 2015, undergraduate level, instructor Cauam Ferreira Cardoso.","source_span":"11.005 | Spring 2015 | Undergraduate\nIntroduction to International Development"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_11-005-introduction-to-international-dev_assignments","statement":"The final project can be developed independently or in groups of 2 or 3 students.","source_span":"Develop a final project independently, or in groups of 2 or 3."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_11-005-introduction-to-international-dev_assignments","statement":"The final project has three components: an outline created from a final project outline template PDF, a class presentation, and a final paper.","source_span":"The project consists of an outline (created from this\nfinal project outline template (PDF)\n), a class presentation, and a final paper."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_11-005-introduction-to-international-dev_assignments","statement":"An individually written final project paper must be 2,500 to 3,000 words.","source_span":"If paper is written individually: 2,500 to 3,000-word document that details the answers to the relevant questions below."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_11-005-introduction-to-international-dev_assignments","statement":"A group-written final project paper must be 5,000–6,000 words.","source_span":"If your project is written as a group: 5,000–6,000 words."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_11-005-introduction-to-international-dev_assignments","statement":"Final project presentations happen during sessions 22 and 24.","source_span":"Presentations will happen during sessions 22 & 24."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_11-005-introduction-to-international-dev_assignments","statement":"Individual presentations are limited to 7 minutes with time for a brief Q&A.","source_span":"If presenting individually, your presentation limit is 7 minutes with time for a brief Q&A."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_11-005-introduction-to-international-dev_assignments","statement":"Group presentations are limited to 14 minutes with time for a brief Q&A.","source_span":"If presenting as a group, your presentation limit is 14 minutes with time for a brief Q&A."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_11-005-introduction-to-international-dev_assignments","statement":"The final paper is due on session 25.","source_span":"The final paper is due on session 25."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d1_11-005-introduction-to-international-dev_assignments","fact_refs":[0,1],"question":"Who instructs 11.005 Introduction to International Development, and can the final project be done in groups?","answer":"Cauam Ferreira Cardoso instructs the course, and the final project can be developed independently or in groups of 2 or 3 students."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d1_11-005-introduction-to-international-dev_assignments","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What three components make up the final project in Introduction to International Development?","answer":"The final project consists of an outline (created from a final project outline template PDF), a class presentation, and a final paper."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d1_11-005-introduction-to-international-dev_assignments","fact_refs":[3,4],"question":"What is the required word count for the final project paper, and does it differ for individual versus group submissions?","answer":"Yes — an individually written paper must be 2,500 to 3,000 words, while a group-written paper must be 5,000–6,000 words."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d1_11-005-introduction-to-international-dev_assignments","fact_refs":[5],"question":"During which sessions do final project presentations take place in Introduction to International Development?","answer":"Final project presentations happen during sessions 22 and 24."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d1_11-005-introduction-to-international-dev_assignments","fact_refs":[6,7],"question":"How long are individual versus group final project presentations in this course?","answer":"Individual presentations are limited to 7 minutes plus a brief Q&A, while group presentations are limited to 14 minutes plus a brief Q&A."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d1_11-005-introduction-to-international-dev_assignments","fact_refs":[8],"question":"When is the final paper due in Introduction to International Development?","answer":"The final paper is due on session 25."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_11-005-introduction-to-international-dev_calendar","statement":"Session 1 is an introductory welcome class that reviews the syllabus, class requirements, and identifies key data sources and competencies for the semester.","source_span":"The welcome class will be an overview of the course. We'll start with introductions, discuss the objectives and expectations for the class, and review the syllabus and class requirements."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_11-005-introduction-to-international-dev_calendar","statement":"Session 3, on the Ethical Underpinnings of Development, features guest speaker Diego Laserna, an MIT-DUSP alum and City Council candidate for Bogota, Colombia in 2015.","source_span":"Diego Laserna, MIT-DUSP Alum and City Council Candidate for the City of Bogota, Colombia in 2015."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_11-005-introduction-to-international-dev_calendar","statement":"Session 6, on Identities in Development, features guest speakers from the Student of Color Committee DUSP-MIT.","source_span":"Guest Speakers: Student of Color Committee DUSP-MIT."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_11-005-introduction-to-international-dev_calendar","statement":"Session 8 discusses the 1960s and 1970s as the first decades in which long-term development data became available, and how modernization paradigm limitations became increasingly obvious.","source_span":"The 1960s and 1970s represented the first decades in which long-term development data was available. Technological advances provided more computational power as well as better communications."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_11-005-introduction-to-international-dev_calendar","statement":"Session 10 covers the 1980s and 1990s debt crisis, describing Latin America and Africa as experiencing 'lost decades' with structural adjustments enforced by the IMF and World Bank, while Korea and Taiwan promoted growth and prosperity in East Asia.","source_span":"For countries in Latin America and Africa, for example, these were \"lost decades,\" with serious economic crises and eventual structural adjustments enforced by the IMF and the World Bank. On the other hand, in East Asia, countries like Korea and Taiwan managed to promote periods of growth and prosperity."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d1_11-005-introduction-to-international-dev_calendar","statement":"Session 11 examines different views on why and how institutions matter for development, noting that the rise of neoliberalism in the 1980s and 1990s created special attention to the debate over which institutions matter.","source_span":"the rise of neoliberalism in the 1980s and 1990s created a special level of attention to the debate around\nwhich\ninstitutions matter for development, and how countries should go about creating them."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d1_11-005-introduction-to-international-dev_calendar","fact_refs":[1],"question":"Who is the guest speaker for the Session 3 class on the Ethical Underpinnings of Development?","answer":"Diego Laserna, an MIT-DUSP alum and 2015 City Council candidate for Bogota, Colombia, is the guest speaker."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d1_11-005-introduction-to-international-dev_calendar","fact_refs":[2],"question":"Which guest speakers appear in Session 6 on Identities in Development?","answer":"Guest speakers from the Student of Color Committee DUSP-MIT appear in Session 6."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d1_11-005-introduction-to-international-dev_calendar","fact_refs":[4],"question":"According to the course calendar, how did the 1980s and 1990s debt crisis affect Latin America and Africa compared to Korea and Taiwan?","answer":"Latin America and Africa experienced 'lost decades' with serious economic crises and IMF/World Bank-enforced structural adjustments, while Korea and Taiwan managed to promote periods of growth and prosperity in East Asia."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d1_11-005-introduction-to-international-dev_calendar","fact_refs":[3],"question":"Why does the course calendar say the 1960s and 1970s were significant for development data and theory?","answer":"They were the first decades in which long-term development data became available, and with more computational power and communications, the limitations of the modernization paradigm became increasingly obvious."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d1_11-005-introduction-to-international-dev_calendar","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What debate intensified around institutions and development in the 1980s and 1990s, according to Session 11?","answer":"The rise of neoliberalism created special attention to the debate over which institutions matter for development and how countries should go about creating them."}
